                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Played On: [b]HTC Vive[/b][/h1]\n\nThe opening scene of The Dream Collector shows a young man with an electric guitar, on a bridge. He plays a note, hugs it, and then chucks it over the concrete into oblivion. The next moment, his clothes are changed to business attire, complete with tie. This opening sequence resonated strongly with me, as I'm sure it will many, regardless if you had dreams of being a musician or not; the item is merely symbolic.\n\nAs you may have guessed by now, The Dream Collector is a short animated story about a magical man, his dog, and his ability to find, fix-up, and recycle discarded dreams. Whether they be broken guitars, deflated soccer balls, busted baseball mitts, you name it and the man can fix it with the help of some magic. The scenes follow him throughout the seasons, as he collects, fixes, and sends these items to an unknown location. All is revealed in a surprise ending that's definitely worth sticking around for.\n\nEven though this title is only 12 minutes long, the graphics, characters, environments, soundtrack, and unnarrated story are all well thought out, strong, and extremely enjoyable for people of all ages. The attention to detail in each setting is commendable, and the interactive film is complete with rain, snow, and sun effects; it almost feels like it could be a Pixar movie. The regular price of $2.29 CAD is suiting for The Dream Collector, and if you manage to grab it on sale it's an even better deal. It's a great seasonal title, and would be worth a yearly watch and also to show off to family and friends around the holidays.\n\n[h1]Rating: [b]4.0/5.0[/b] - Excellent, highly worth playing.[/h1]\nThe VR Network [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/33182845-The-VR-Network/]Curator[/url] | [url=https://steamcommunity.com/groups/thevrnetwork]Group[/url] Click for More",
